    statement:
      "A job runner's config file lets people write timeouts as `1h30m` instead of counting seconds. Implement `parse_duration(text)`.\n\nRules:\n- Leading and trailing whitespace is ignored.\n- A value made only of the digits `0-9` (for example `\"90\"`) is a number of seconds.\n- Otherwise the value is one or more `<digits><unit>` parts written back to back with nothing between them. The units are `d` (24 hours), `h`, `m` and `s`. Each unit may appear **at most once**, and the parts must come in the order `d`, `h`, `m`, `s` (any of them may be left out).\n- Return the total number of seconds as an `int`.\n- Return `None` for anything else: an empty string, an unknown unit, a unit with no digits in front of it, digits with no unit after a unit part (`\"1h30\"`), a repeated or out-of-order unit, a sign, a decimal point, or a space inside the value.",
    functionName: "parse_duration",
    functionSignature: "def parse_duration(text: str):",
    buggyCode:
      "import re\n\ndef parse_duration(text: str):\n    \"\"\"Turn \"1d2h3m4s\"-style text (or bare seconds like \"90\") into a number of seconds.\n    Units d, h, m, s: each at most once, in that order. Surrounding whitespace is ignored.\n    Return None for anything that does not follow the format.\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n",
    solution:
      "import re\n\ndef parse_duration(text: str):\n    text = text.strip()\n    if re.fullmatch(r'[0-9]+', text):\n        return int(text)\n    m = re.fullmatch(r'(?:([0-9]+)d)?(?:([0-9]+)h)?(?:([0-9]+)m)?(?:([0-9]+)s)?', text)\n    if not text or not m:\n        return None\n    days, hours, minutes, seconds = (int(g) if g else 0 for g in m.groups())\n    return ((days * 24 + hours) * 60 + minutes) * 60 + seconds\n",
    hint: "One anchored pattern with four optional groups in a fixed order rejects repeats and wrong orders for free. Remember that a pattern made only of optional groups also matches the empty string.",
    explanation:
      "The format is a tiny grammar: four optional `<digits><unit>` groups in a fixed order. Scanning for every `<digits><unit>` pair and summing them is the tempting approach, but it happily accepts `30m1h`, `1h1h` and `1h30` because it never checks what lies between or around the pairs. Matching the whole string against one anchored pattern (or walking the string once while remembering the last unit seen) makes order, repeats and leftovers impossible to miss. Handle the two special cases first: a bare run of digits is seconds, and the empty string must be rejected because an all-optional pattern matches it. Time and space are O(n) in the length of the text.",
