      "A search endpoint receives its filters as a raw query string. Implement `parse_query(qs)` and return a dict.\n\nRules:\n- One leading `?` is dropped if present.\n- The string is split into pieces on `&`. Empty pieces are skipped.\n- Each piece is split at its **first** `=` into a key and a value. A piece with no `=` has the value `\"\"`.\n- After splitting, the key and the value are each decoded: `+` becomes a space, then every `%XX` escape is percent-decoded as UTF-8 (`urllib.parse.unquote_plus` does exactly this). Test inputs only contain well-formed escapes.\n- A piece whose decoded key is empty is skipped.\n- A key that appears once maps to its string value. A key that appears more than once maps to a list of all its values in the order they appear.",
    functionName: "parse_query",
    functionSignature: "def parse_query(qs: str) -> dict:",
    buggyCode:
      "from urllib.parse import unquote_plus\n\ndef parse_query(qs: str) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Parse a raw query string into a dict.\n    Drop one leading \"?\", split on \"&\", split each piece at its first \"=\",\n    then decode \"+\" and %XX in keys and values. Skip empty pieces and empty keys.\n    A repeated key maps to a list of its values in order; otherwise to a string.\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n",
    solution:
      "from urllib.parse import unquote_plus\n\ndef parse_query(qs: str) -> dict:\n    if qs.startswith('?'):\n        qs = qs[1:]\n    out = {}\n    for piece in qs.split('&'):\n        if not piece:\n            continue\n        raw_key, _, raw_value = piece.partition('=')\n        key = unquote_plus(raw_key)\n        if key == '':\n            continue\n        value = unquote_plus(raw_value)\n        if key not in out:\n            out[key] = value\n        elif isinstance(out[key], list):\n            out[key].append(value)\n        else:\n            out[key] = [out[key], value]\n    return out\n",
    hint: "Split first, decode second: an encoded `%26` or `%3D` inside a value must not be treated as a separator. `str.partition('=')` splits at the first `=` only.",
    explanation:
      "Query strings are split on structure first and decoded afterwards. If you decode the whole string up front, an escaped `&` or `=` inside a value turns into a separator and corrupts the result; if you use `split('=')` and take the second element, a value such as a base64 token ending in `==` is cut short. `partition('=')` gives the key, and everything after the first `=` as the value, and an empty value when there is no `=` at all. Collecting repeats needs a small three-way branch: first sighting stores a string, second sighting upgrades it to a list, later sightings append. The work is a single pass, O(n) time and space in the length of the string.",
