    statement:
      "A notification service fills message templates from a JSON payload. Implement `fill_template(template, values)` and return the finished text.\n\nA placeholder is `{{`, some text, then the nearest following `}}` (placeholders never span lines). Inside it:\n- The text before the first `|` is a **path**: keys joined by dots, looked up step by step through nested dicts in `values` (`user.name` means `values[\"user\"][\"name\"]`). Whitespace around the path is ignored.\n- If there is a `|`, everything after the first one, with surrounding whitespace removed, is the **default** text. It may be empty and may itself contain `|`.\n\nFor each placeholder:\n- If the path leads to a value that is not `None`, the placeholder is replaced by `str(value)`. Values are strings or integers, and `0` and `\"\"` are real values.\n- Otherwise the path is missing (a key is absent, a step goes through something that is not a dict, or the value is `None`). Use the default if the placeholder has one; if it has none, leave the placeholder in the output exactly as it was written.\n\nInserted text is never scanned again for placeholders. A `{{` with no `}}` after it is ordinary text.",
    functionName: "fill_template",
    functionSignature: "def fill_template(template: str, values: dict) -> str:",
    buggyCode:
      "import re\n\ndef fill_template(template: str, values: dict) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Replace {{ path }} and {{ path | default }} placeholders in template.\n    path is dot-separated keys into nested dicts in values. A found, non-None value is\n    inserted with str(). A missing path uses the default if one is given, otherwise the\n    placeholder is left untouched. Inserted text is not scanned again.\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n",
    solution:
      "import re\n\ndef fill_template(template: str, values: dict) -> str:\n    def lookup(path):\n        node = values\n        for part in path.split('.'):\n            if not isinstance(node, dict) or part not in node:\n                return None\n            node = node[part]\n        return node\n\n    def swap(match):\n        path, bar, default = match.group(1).partition('|')\n        found = lookup(path.strip())\n        if found is not None:\n            return str(found)\n        if bar:\n            return default.strip()\n        return match.group(0)\n\n    return re.sub(r'\\{\\{(.*?)\\}\\}', swap, template)\n",
    hint: "`re.sub` accepts a function as the replacement, which gives you one pass over the template with no re-scanning. Compare the looked-up value against None, not against truthiness.",
    explanation:
      "Templating is a single left-to-right pass: find each placeholder, decide its replacement, and move on past the inserted text. Doing it with repeated `str.replace` calls is the naive route and it breaks in two ways: text that was just inserted can be mistaken for another placeholder, and the same placeholder text is replaced everywhere at once. A non-greedy pattern with a replacement function handles both. The lookup walks the dotted path one key at a time and bails out with `None` as soon as a step is absent or the current node is not a dict. The decision must test `is not None`, because `0` and the empty string are legitimate values that a truthiness test would silently replace with the default. Cost is O(n + total path length) time and O(n) space for the output.",
