      "A deploy tool stores settings as flat rows such as `db.pool.size = 10` and the app wants them as a nested dict. Implement `nest_keys(pairs)`.\n\n`pairs` is a list of `[key, value]` rows processed **in order**. A key is one or more non-empty names joined by dots; every name before the last one is a nested dict, and the last name holds the value. Values are strings, numbers, booleans or `None`, never dicts.\n\n- A later row with exactly the same key replaces the earlier value.\n- A row **conflicts** when it would need a dict where a plain value is already stored (`a.b = 1` then `a.b.c = 2`), or would store a plain value where a dict already exists (`a.b.c = 1` then `a.b = 2`).\n- At the first conflicting row, stop and return `{\"conflict\": <that row's full key>}`.\n- With no conflicts, return `{\"config\": <the nested dict>}` (an empty dict for no rows).",
    functionName: "nest_keys",
    functionSignature: "def nest_keys(pairs: list) -> dict:",
    buggyCode:
      "def nest_keys(pairs: list) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Build a nested dict from [dotted_key, value] rows processed in order.\n    Same key again: the later value wins. A row that needs a dict where a plain value\n    is stored, or a plain value where a dict is stored, is a conflict.\n    Return {\"conflict\": key} for the first conflicting row, else {\"config\": nested}.\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n",
    solution:
      "def nest_keys(pairs: list) -> dict:\n    root = {}\n    for key, value in pairs:\n        parts = key.split('.')\n        node = root\n        for part in parts[:-1]:\n            if part not in node:\n                node[part] = {}\n            elif not isinstance(node[part], dict):\n                return {'conflict': key}\n            node = node[part]\n        if isinstance(node.get(parts[-1]), dict):\n            return {'conflict': key}\n        node[parts[-1]] = value\n    return {'config': root}\n",
    hint: "Walk down one name at a time, creating dicts as you go. There are two different places a row can collide: on the way down, and at the final name.",
    explanation:
      "Each key is a path: descend through the names before the last one, creating an empty dict whenever a name is new, then assign at the last name. The naive version uses `setdefault(part, {})` all the way down and then assigns, which either crashes or silently destroys data when the shapes disagree. There are exactly two collision points. While descending, the name may already hold a plain value, so there is nothing to descend into. At the final name, a dict may already live there, and overwriting it would throw away a whole subtree. Because values are never dicts, an `isinstance(..., dict)` test tells the two apart reliably. Rows are handled in order and the first conflict ends the run. Time is O(total number of key parts); space is the size of the result.",
