      "A report scheduler uses a three-field cron-like spec. Implement `next_runs(spec, after, count)` and return the first `count` run times **strictly later** than `after`, in ascending order.\n\n`spec` is `\"MINUTE HOUR WEEKDAY\"`, three fields separated by single spaces. Allowed values are minute `0-59`, hour `0-23`, and weekday `0-6` where **`0` is Monday** and `6` is Sunday (the same numbering as Python's `date.weekday()`).\n\nEach field is a comma-separated list of terms, and a field matches the union of its terms:\n- `*` is every allowed value.\n- `N` is the single value N.\n- `A-B` is every value from A to B inclusive.\n- `*/S` or `A-B/S` takes every S-th value **counting from the start of the range**: `*/15` in the minute field is 0, 15, 30, 45 and `9-17/4` is 9, 13, 17.\n\nA job runs at every minute whose minute, hour and weekday all match. `after` and every returned time use the format `\"YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM\"`. The spec is always valid, and `count` may be `0`.",
