    statement:
      "A product-page cache serves slightly old data while it refreshes in the background. Implement `swr_cache(ttl, grace, ops)`, a simulation of that policy.\n\n`ops` is a list of reads `[t, key]` with non-decreasing integer times `t`. The cache remembers, for each key, the time it was last loaded. Each read gets one label:\n- `\"miss\"`: the key has never been loaded. It is loaded now.\n- Otherwise let `age = t - loaded_at`:\n  - `age < ttl` is `\"fresh\"`. Nothing changes.\n  - `ttl <= age < ttl + grace` is `\"stale\"`. The old copy is served and a refresh runs, so the key counts as loaded at time `t`.\n  - `age >= ttl + grace` is `\"expired\"`. The copy is too old to serve and the key is loaded again at time `t`.\n\nReturn `{\"labels\": [one label per read, in order], \"loads\": n}` where `n` counts every load: each `\"miss\"`, `\"stale\"` and `\"expired\"` read is one load.",
    functionName: "swr_cache",
    functionSignature: "def swr_cache(ttl: int, grace: int, ops: list) -> dict:",
    buggyCode:
      "def swr_cache(ttl: int, grace: int, ops: list) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Simulate a stale-while-revalidate cache over reads [t, key] (t non-decreasing).\n    Never loaded -> \"miss\". Else age = t - loaded_at: age < ttl -> \"fresh\";\n    age < ttl + grace -> \"stale\"; otherwise \"expired\".\n    miss, stale and expired each (re)load the key at time t and count as one load.\n    Return {\"labels\": [...], \"loads\": n}.\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n",
    solution:
      "def swr_cache(ttl: int, grace: int, ops: list) -> dict:\n    loaded = {}\n    labels = []\n    loads = 0\n    for t, key in ops:\n        if key not in loaded:\n            label = 'miss'\n        else:\n            age = t - loaded[key]\n            if age < ttl:\n                label = 'fresh'\n            elif age < ttl + grace:\n                label = 'stale'\n            else:\n                label = 'expired'\n        if label != 'fresh':\n            loaded[key] = t\n            loads += 1\n        labels.append(label)\n    return {'labels': labels, 'loads': loads}\n",
    hint: "Work out the label first, from the age at the moment of the read. Then apply one shared rule: every label except \"fresh\" resets the key's load time to t.",
    explanation:
      "Stale-while-revalidate splits an entry's life into three bands measured from its last load: fresh up to the TTL, stale for a grace period after that, and expired beyond it. Both boundaries are half-open, so an age exactly equal to `ttl` is already stale and an age exactly equal to `ttl + grace` is already expired. The detail that is easiest to miss is that a stale read triggers a refresh: the reader gets the old copy, but the entry's clock restarts, so the next read shortly afterwards is fresh again. If the stale branch does not reset the load time, the key drifts into expired and callers see slow reads that the policy was designed to prevent. Classify first and update second so the label always reflects the state before the read. One pass with a dict: O(n) time, O(k) space for k distinct keys.",
