    functionSignature: "def build_tree(rows: list) -> dict:",
    buggyCode:
      "def build_tree(rows: list) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Turn flat rows {\"id\", \"parent\", \"name\", \"size\"} into a forest.\n    Roots: parent is None or names an id that is not in rows. Node shape:\n    {\"id\", \"name\", \"total\": own size + totals of all children, \"children\": [...]}.\n    Roots and children are sorted by (name, id). Rows unreachable from any root (parent\n    loops and whatever hangs below them) go into \"dropped\" as sorted ids.\n    Return {\"roots\": [...], \"dropped\": [...]}.\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n",
    solution:
      "def build_tree(rows: list) -> dict:\n    by_id = {row['id']: row for row in rows}\n    kids = {}\n    top = []\n    for row in rows:\n        if row['parent'] is None or row['parent'] not in by_id:\n            top.append(row)\n        else:\n            kids.setdefault(row['parent'], []).append(row)\n    seen = set()\n\n    def order(items):\n        return sorted(items, key=lambda r: (r['name'], r['id']))\n\n    def make(row):\n        seen.add(row['id'])\n        children = [make(child) for child in order(kids.get(row['id'], []))]\n        total = row['size'] + sum(child['total'] for child in children)\n        return {'id': row['id'], 'name': row['name'], 'total': total, 'children': children}\n\n    roots = [make(row) for row in order(top)]\n    return {'roots': roots, 'dropped': sorted(i for i in by_id if i not in seen)}\n",
    hint: "Index the rows by id and group them by parent in one pass. Build nodes from the roots downward, recording every id you visit: whatever was never visited is the dropped list.",
    explanation:
      "Converting an adjacency list to a tree takes two passes. The first builds an id index and a parent-to-children map, deciding for each row whether it is a root: either it has no parent, or its parent id is not in the index. The second builds nodes recursively from the roots, sorting each group of siblings and computing `total` on the way back up, so every subtree is summed exactly once. Adding only the direct children's own sizes is the usual slip and undercounts anything nested deeper than one level. Loops need no dedicated detection. A row inside a parent loop is never a root and never the descendant of one, so a top-down build simply does not reach it; recording visited ids and subtracting them from the full set yields the dropped list. Attempting to find each row's root by following parent links upward, in contrast, runs forever on a loop. Total cost is O(n log n) for the sibling sorts and O(n) space.",
