    topic: "Inventory & Scheduling",
    statement:
      "A wine merchant buys the same bottle at different prices over time and, for accounting, treats the oldest bottles as the first ones sold. Implement `fifo_cost(ops)`.\n\n`ops` is processed in order:\n- `[\"buy\", qty, unit_cents]` adds a lot of `qty` bottles that cost `unit_cents` each. Its result is `None`.\n- `[\"sell\", qty]` removes `qty` bottles, taking them from the **oldest** lot that still has bottles and moving on to the next lot when one runs out. Its result is the total cost in cents of the bottles removed.\n- A sale for more bottles than are on hand is rejected: its result is `-1` and no bottles are removed.\n\nAll quantities are positive integers. Return `{\"results\": [one per op], \"on_hand\": <bottles left>, \"value\": <total cost in cents of the bottles left>}`.",
    functionName: "fifo_cost",
    functionSignature: "def fifo_cost(ops: list) -> dict:",
    buggyCode:
      "def fifo_cost(ops: list) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Process [\"buy\", qty, unit_cents] and [\"sell\", qty] in order with first-in-first-out lots.\n    buy -> result None. sell -> cost in cents of the units taken from the oldest lots first,\n    or -1 (and nothing is taken) when there is not enough stock.\n    Return {\"results\": [...], \"on_hand\": units left, \"value\": cost of the units left}.\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n",
    solution:
      "def fifo_cost(ops: list) -> dict:\n    lots = []  # [qty_left, unit_cents], oldest first\n    results = []\n    for op in ops:\n        if op[0] == 'buy':\n            lots.append([op[1], op[2]])\n            results.append(None)\n            continue\n        need = op[1]\n        if need > sum(qty for qty, _ in lots):\n            results.append(-1)\n            continue\n        cost = 0\n        while need > 0:\n            take = min(need, lots[0][0])\n            cost += take * lots[0][1]\n            lots[0][0] -= take\n            need -= take\n            if lots[0][0] == 0:\n                lots.pop(0)\n        results.append(cost)\n    return {\n        'results': results,\n        'on_hand': sum(qty for qty, _ in lots),\n        'value': sum(qty * unit for qty, unit in lots),\n    }\n",
    hint: "Keep the lots in a queue, oldest first, each with its remaining quantity. Check that the whole sale can be filled before you take anything from a lot.",
    explanation:
      "FIFO costing keeps purchase lots in a queue. A sale drains the front lot, moves to the next one when it is empty, and adds up `taken * unit_cost` across the lots it touches, so one sale can span several prices. The important ordering is validate first, mutate second: compare the requested quantity with the total on hand before consuming anything. Consuming while checking leaves the inventory half-drained when a sale turns out to be too large, and every later result is then wrong. A weighted-average shortcut gives different numbers as soon as lots have different prices, and needs division that integers cannot always do exactly. What remains at the end is described by the same queue: the units left and their value at original cost. With a list, removing the front lot is O(lots); `collections.deque` makes it O(1). Overall time is O(ops + lots) apart from the on-hand sum per sale, which a running counter would remove.",
